[Music]  one of the questions I get asked a lot  is what screenwriting books should I buy  what one is the best but I actually  think that's the wrong question to ask  because the answer I give is entirely  dependent on one thing what your  weakness is as a writer because it's  only by working on our weaknesses that  we can improve let's say you struggle  with character  they aren't believable or feel  mechanical also just a different book  than if your problem was structure or  plot so in this video I want to break  down some of my favorite screenwriting  books and the reasons I'd recommend them  and stay tuned because at the end I'll  give my opinion for the best thing you  can be reading as a screenwriter  the first is Sid fields screenplay often  consider the bible of screenwriting  books  it's an in-depth analysis of 3x  structure and a fantastic starting point  for beginners or those who struggle with  plot and structure now I've been  noticing a paradigm shift away from 3x  structure in the last few years  particularly in the TV sphere but this  method is still dominant and as such  every screenwriter should have a firm  understanding of it even if they don't  intend to use it  similar to screenplay and one of my  personal favorites is Robert McKee's  story I usually recommend this as the  second book to read after screenplay  because they complement each other it's  a little less accessible but the stuff  in here is fantastic with greater  expansion on three-act structure some  character work and useful examples of  how his techniques applied to films we  know and love and then there's what's  commonly referred to as the people  magazine of screenwriting Blake Snyder's  save the cat if you want to write  mainstream high concept blockbusters  this is the book for you it's an  entertaining read that introduces some  interesting concepts that I still use  like Pope in the pool or double  mumbo-jumbo if you don't know what those  things are check this book out now I  want to get into some books I'd  recommend if you struggle with character  character development and to some extent  dialogue because dialogue is  intrinsically linked to character often  if you have trouble with dialogue it's  rooted in a problem with the character  the first book is km Wyland's creating  character arc  this is one of my absolute favorites so  much so that I did an entire video using  her concepts and applied it to Star Wars  in it you'll learn about the three  different types of character arcs and  how a character shapes the story not the  other way around it's also a great  analysis of how your character changes  over the course of the film or how they  change the world around them highly  recommend the second is Lagos agrees the  art of dramatic writing now this is  actually a book about playwriting but a  lot of the stuff in here is transferable  for us screenwriters chiefly among them  is the idea of the tri-dimensional  character in which he argues that in  order to understand how characters react  in certain situations we must first know  three things about them there's  sociology physiology and psychology so  if you've ever gotten the note oh I  don't think the character would do that  or your characters aren't believable I'd  check out this book then there are the  books I consider outliers the first is  John troubies the anatomy of story which  offers a different approach to  storytelling that as a direct refutation  of the traditional three act structure  tired of McKee or field this is the book  for you offering new paradigm shifting  techniques on how to approach story I  also love how truly uses multiple films  as example throughout to highlight his  tools and concepts then there's  Christopher Vogler's the writer's  journey which looks at storytelling from  a place of heroic myth a character goes  into the unknown battles his or her  demons and comes back a hero the  interesting thing about the writers  journey is that it can fit in almost any  of the affer mentioned structures this  book is also great if you're stuck say  there's a problem with your script and  you just can't figure it out going  through the steps of the writers journey  can help pinpoint where your problem is  and offer solutions on how to solve it  last and certainly not least the best  thing you can be reading to better  yourself as a writer is to read other  scripts read bad ones read good ones  immerse yourself in other people's work  you'll start learning what you like what  you don't pay seing tone and other  things you can't learn from watching the  movie or reading a book as someone who  has read over a thousand scripts I can  honestly say that reading others has  been one of the greatest learning  periences I'll leave a link below where  you can find out how to access some of  these scripts and I also suggest  checking out the annual blacklist to  give you a sense of what is out there  and what is getting buzz so that's my  breakdown of my favorite screenwriting  books what are yours let me know in the  comments below I'd love your feedback  and as always thanks for watching don't  forget to Like and subscribe and hit  that Bell button keep on writing

